error[ffi_writable_parameter]: `t` of `tally_bump` is lent read-only as a `Tally`, and `ffi-a-record-lent-read-only-past-a-variadic-prototype.h` gives `tally_bump` 1 parameter and then `...`, where `t` stands — C reads what a call hands it there as whatever type it asks for, so nothing holds it to the `const` the lend promises
  at tests/golden/unsupported/ffi-a-record-lent-read-only-past-a-variadic-prototype.hero:15:33
     |
  15 |     function tally_bump(k: i32, t: Tally lent) -> i64  #~ ffi_writable_parameter
     |                                 ^
  note: spec § 13: a group's record declared `lent` without `@` is a pointer to const, the place C reads and never writes. C11 6.5.2.2p7: past a prototype's `...` a call converts nothing, and `tally_bump` reads the pointer as the type its `va_arg` names, so neither clang nor this compiler can hold C to the `const`, and a write there changes a value the program declared unchanged
  note: write a header of your own that includes `ffi-a-record-lent-read-only-past-a-variadic-prototype.h` and defines a `static inline` function of a new name with a fixed prototype, taking `t` as a pointer to `const` of the struct `tally_bump` reads and calling `tally_bump` with it; bind that function instead, and its prototype holds the `const`; where `tally_bump` reads a format, write the format inside that function as a literal, and clang reads each conversion against the argument it is given
